## How to Install Datadog on Kubernetes

### 1️⃣ Create Datadog API Key Secret
```bash
kubectl create secret generic datadog-secret \
  --from-literal api-key=<DATADOG_API_KEY> \
  -n datadog
2️⃣ Install Datadog Agent using Helm
bash
Copy code
helm repo add datadog https://helm.datadoghq.com
helm repo update

helm install datadog datadog/datadog \
  --namespace datadog --create-namespace \
  --set datadog.apiKeyExistingSecret=datadog-secret \
  --set datadog.site=datadoghq.com \
  --set kubeStateMetricsEnabled=true
3️⃣ Enable APM (Optional)
yaml
Copy code
datadog:
  apm:
    enabled: true
4️⃣ Enable Logs Collection
yaml
Copy code
datadog:
  logs:
    enabled: true
    containerCollectAll: true
Best Practices
Use tags (env, team, service) consistently

Avoid alerting on single metrics—use composite alerts

Track golden signals: latency, traffic, errors, saturation

Set different alert thresholds for dev vs prod

Periodically review dashboards and alerts for relevance

Common Dashboards We Maintain
Kubernetes Cluster Overview

Namespace Resource Usage

Service Latency & Error Rate

Deployment Health

SLO & Error Budget Dashboard

Security Considerations
API keys stored in Kubernetes Secrets

RBAC applied to limit dashboard and alert changes

Logs and metrics encrypted in transit

PII masking enabled where applicable

When NOT to Use Datadog Alone
Long-term audit log retention (use S3/ELK)

Deep packet inspection

Custom ML-based business analytics

Datadog works best as a real-time observability layer, not a data warehouse.

Summary
Datadog enables full-stack observability across Kubernetes, cloud infrastructure, and applications.
By correlating metrics, logs, and traces, it significantly improves incident response, reliability, and operational efficiency, making it a core platform for DevOps and SRE teams.
